<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="798" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Should You Demand Appraisal? A Michigan Decision Checklist</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Guide · 7 min read · November 10, 2026</p><h1>Should You Demand Appraisal? A Michigan Decision Checklist</h1><p class="ph-lede">A practical checklist for deciding whether appraisal fits your disputed claim, and the signs that another path is better.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Appraisal is a tool, not a reflex</h2>
<p>Appraisal can resolve a stuck Michigan property claim faster and more cheaply than litigation, but it isn’t right for every dispute. Demanding it too early, on the wrong kind of disagreement or on a small gap can cost more than it recovers. This checklist walks through the questions worth answering before you send a demand. It’s general information, not legal advice.</p>
<h2>Question 1: Is the dispute about amount or coverage?</h2>
<p>Michigan’s Insurance Code, at MCL 500.2833, requires property policies to include an appraisal provision for when the parties fail to agree on the actual cash value or amount of loss. That’s its scope. If the insurer says the loss is covered but worth less than you think, appraisal fits. If the insurer says the loss isn’t covered at all, because of wear and tear, surface water, a late notice or another exclusion, that’s a coverage question for a Michigan attorney.</p>
<p>Many claims are partly both. An insurer might accept roof damage on two slopes but deny the rest as old. Appraisal can value the accepted portion, while the coverage question for the rest is handled separately.</p>
<h2>Question 2: How big is the gap?</h2>
<p>Compare the insurer’s estimate with a credible contractor bid or independent estimate. The larger the documented gap, the more sense appraisal makes. You’ll usually pay your own appraiser and half the umpire fee, so a small gap can be eaten up by costs.</p>
<p>In an illustrative example, a $4,000 gap on a fence and gutter claim may be better handled by sending the adjuster a contractor bid and photos. A $35,000 gap on a fire-damaged kitchen and smoke-affected house is a much stronger appraisal candidate. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> lets you test your numbers.</p>
<h2>Question 3: Have you given the insurer a fair chance?</h2>
<p>Before demanding appraisal, make sure the insurer has your documents: photos, contractor bids, invoices, mitigation reports and contents lists. Ask in writing for a revised estimate that addresses specific items. Sometimes an adjuster simply missed something. If the insurer still won’t move, appraisal becomes a reasonable next step.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep it in writing</b>Phone calls with adjusters are useful, but follow each one with a short email that summarizes what was said. A clear paper trail helps in appraisal and in any later dispute.</div>
<h2>Question 4: Is your documentation ready?</h2>
<ul>
<li>A complete copy of the policy, declarations page and endorsements.</li>
<li>All insurer estimates and letters.</li>
<li>At least one detailed contractor bid, ideally line by line.</li>
<li>Date-stamped photos and video of the damage.</li>
<li>Receipts for mitigation and temporary repairs.</li>
<li>Contents lists with ages and approximate costs, if contents are in dispute.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/appraisal-readiness-score/">appraisal readiness score</a> walks you through these items quickly.</p>
<h2>Question 5: Where do your deadlines stand?</h2>
<p>Michigan generally requires suit on a property claim within one year after the loss, or longer if the policy allows, with the period tolled from when you give notice until the insurer formally denies liability. Appraisal doesn’t necessarily pause that clock. If a deadline is close or unclear, talk with a Michigan attorney before relying on appraisal alone. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/claim-deadline-planner/">claim deadline planner</a> helps keep dates organized.</p>
<h2>Question 6: Can you find an independent appraiser?</h2>
<p>The statute calls for a competent, independent appraiser on each side. Avoid anyone with a financial stake in the repairs, such as the contractor who will do the work. Ask about fee structure, prior work for your insurer and experience with your type of loss, whether that’s a tornado-damaged roof in Gaylord, an ice dam in Marquette or a basement backup in Dearborn.</p>
<h2>Red flags that point elsewhere</h2>
<ol>
<li>The insurer has denied coverage outright.</li>
<li>You’ve received a reservation of rights letter or a request for an examination under oath.</li>
<li>The suit deadline is approaching.</li>
<li>The gap is small compared with likely fees.</li>
<li>The main issue is how the claim was handled, such as delays or alleged unfair practices.</li>
</ol>
<p>For delays and handling concerns, Michigan’s Uniform Trade Practices Act prohibits unfair claim settlement practices, and MCL 500.2006 provides for 12% annual penalty interest on certain late claims. Neither is a guarantee, and appraisal doesn’t award them. A Michigan attorney or a complaint to DIFS may be the better route.</p>
<h2>If the answers point to appraisal</h2>
<p>If the dispute is about the amount, the gap is meaningful, your file is ready and your deadlines are under control, appraisal is likely worth considering. Send a clear written demand that cites the appraisal provision, then name your appraiser within the time the policy and statute allow. If you’d like help, our <a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> service starts with a free review, and we’ll tell you honestly if appraisal isn’t a fit.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/michigan-appraisal-clause-explained/"><small>Guide</small><span>The Michigan Appraisal Clause Explained in Plain English</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/appraiser-public-adjuster-or-attorney/"><small>Guide</small><span>Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Attorney: Who Fits Your Claim?</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/ice-dam-claims-in-michigan/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Dam Claims in Michigan: Roofs, Ceilings and Disputes</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article>
